<p><strong>A compromised gateway can speak for its whole attestor lane.</strong> Restrict both namespace and conversation scope. The local development transport accepts a caller-declared supported subject; it does not independently authenticate the sender.</p>
<h3>Chart-aligned mapping excerpt</h3>
<p>This is part of <code>broker.yaml</code>, not a complete configuration. The all-conversations selector below is intentionally broad; narrow it for your deployment.</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Illustrative broker identity excerpt"><code>identities:
  - uid: 65533
    principal: chat-gateway
    actor: { type: service, principal: chat-gateway }
    attestor:
      namespaces: [slack.t0123abcd]
      chatScopes:
        - kind: slack
          transport: workspace-slack
          conversation: { kind: any }
identityMappings:
  - subject: slack.t0123abcd.u0123abcd
    principal: alice</code>
</pre>
</section>
<section id="policy">
<h2>Alice needs two grants.</h2>
<p>Permission to prompt <code>pr-commenter</code> does not permit a GitHub write. A separate rule permits the comment capability only through that agent and gateway.</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Illustrative Cedar policy excerpt"><code>@id("alice-prompt")
permit(principal == Dekopon::Principal::"alice",
       action == Dekopon::Action::"agent.prompt",
       resource == Dekopon::Agent::"pr-commenter")
when { context has via &amp;&amp; context.via == "chat-gateway" };

@id("alice-comment")
permit(principal == Dekopon::Principal::"alice",
       action == Dekopon::Action::"gh.pull-request.comment",
       resource == Dekopon::Provider::"gh")
when { context has agent &amp;&amp; context.agent == "pr-commenter"
    &amp;&amp; context has via &amp;&amp; context.via == "chat-gateway" };</code>
</pre>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Request comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Request</th>
<th scope="col">Expected decision with these rules</th>
<th scope="col">Why</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Alice prompts pr-commenter via chat-gateway</th>
<td data-label="Expected decision with these rules">Allow</td>
<td data-label="Why">Matches alice-prompt</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Alice comments through pr-commenter</th>
<td data-label="Expected decision with these rules">Allow, if the capability is loaded and constrained</td>
<td data-label="Why">Matches alice-comment; HTTP still has separate checks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Alice requests merge</th>
<td data-label="Expected decision with these rules">Deny</td>
<td data-label="Why">No matching permit; no merge invocation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>These are worked decisions, not a deployed test. Cedar evaluates static rules, not intent; a permitted comment can still contain harmful text. A model can help draft configuration, but runtime decisions use the saved files.</p>
<p>The provider resource is <code>gh</code>, not an individual repository. This rule does not inspect arbitrary provider input. Restrict the upstream GitHub token’s repository access rather than assuming this policy does so.</p>
</section>
<section id="constraints">
<h2>A Cedar allow does not widen HTTP access.</h2>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Owner-controlled constraint comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Owner-controlled constraint</th>
<th scope="col">Enforced independently of policy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Destination + method</th>
<td data-label="Enforced independently of policy">For the comment example: api.github.com and GET/POST, not arbitrary hosts or methods.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Call / byte / time ceilings</th>
<td data-label="Enforced independently of policy">The provider may need a pre-read plus a write; every call consumes the invocation budget. Host ceilings still cap configured values.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Loaded components</th>
<td data-label="Enforced independently of policy">Use strict: true to refuse startup on policy actions whose providers are not loaded. Default non-strict mode warns; absent capabilities still cannot execute.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Catalog descriptions tell the model what an agent is for; they do not authorize it. <a href="{{ source }}/charts/dekopon/values-pr-summarizer-linter.yaml">The complete pinned chart configuration ↗</a> shows catalog, mapping, constraints and both policy layers together. It requires operator-specific chat/model inputs and compatible providers; it is not a copy-and-run recipe.</p>
</section>
<section id="credentials">
<h2>The native HTTP sink sends the credential.</h2>
<p>A fixed credential binding, optionally selected per agent, stays in broker configuration. The guest builds its request without receiving the value; the native HTTP host injects it for the bound destination. That endpoint receives the credential.</p>
<p>A caller-named public DRN uses an additional <code>secret.use</code> policy and private binding for the exact sink, method and path/query scope. A DRN pasted into ordinary provider input is just text. Resolution is pinned to the invocation, without stale fallback.</p>
<p><strong>Pick endpoints you trust.</strong> Raw-secret response checks cannot stop an authorized endpoint from encoding or transforming the credential it received. A secret binding restricts where that secret goes; it does not narrow every credential-free request the capability can make.</p>
<p><a href="/guides/traces/#records">Check decision and execution evidence</a> · <a href="/deploy/kubernetes/#credentials">Deliver credentials to the correct process</a> · <a href="{{ source }}/docs/secrets.md">Read the exact secret contract ↗</a>
</p>
</section>
